============================================================
4. printf()
============================================================

printf() is used for formatted output.

Syntax:

    printf("format string", arguments);


Example:

    printf("Hello World\n");


Output:

    Hello World


Example:

    int age = 22;

    printf("Age = %d\n", age);


Output:

    Age = 22


============================================================
5. FORMAT STRING
============================================================

The first argument of printf() is usually a format string.

Example:

    printf("Age = %d", age);


Here:

    "Age = %d"

is the format string.

    %d

is a conversion specification.


============================================================
6. IMPORTANT printf() FORMAT SPECIFIERS
============================================================

Common examples:

    %d      signed int
    %i      signed int
    %u      unsigned int
    %ld     long
    %lu     unsigned long
    %lld    long long
    %llu    unsigned long long
    %f      floating-point output
    %c      character
    %s      string
    %p      pointer
    %zu     size_t


Example:

    int age = 22;
    float marks = 85.5f;
    char grade = 'A';

    printf("Age = %d\n", age);
    printf("Marks = %f\n", marks);
    printf("Grade = %c\n", grade);


============================================================
7. printf() WITH MULTIPLE VARIABLES
============================================================

Example:

    int age = 22;
    float marks = 87.5f;
    char grade = 'A';

    printf("Age = %d, Marks = %.2f, Grade = %c\n",
           age, marks, grade);


Output:

    Age = 22, Marks = 87.50, Grade = A


The arguments correspond to the conversion specifications
in order.


============================================================
8. NEWLINE \n
============================================================

    \n

moves the cursor to a new line.

Example:

    printf("Hello\n");
    printf("World\n");


Output:

    Hello
    World


Without newline:

    printf("Hello");
    printf("World");


Output may appear as:

    HelloWorld


