17. getchar()
============================================================

getchar() reads one character from stdin.

Syntax:

    int ch;

    ch = getchar();


Example:

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        int ch;

        printf("Enter a character: ");

        ch = getchar();

        printf("You entered: %c\n", ch);

        return 0;
    }


============================================================
18. WHY getchar() RETURNS int
============================================================

A common interview question.

getchar() returns:

    int

rather than char because it must be able to represent:

    - every possible unsigned char value
    - EOF


EOF means:

    End Of File


Therefore:

    int ch;

is the preferred type for storing getchar()'s result.


============================================================
19. putchar()
============================================================

putchar() writes one character to stdout.

Example:

    putchar('A');


Output:

    A


Example:

    char ch = 'X';

    putchar(ch);


============================================================
20. getchar() + putchar()
============================================================

Example:

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        int ch;

        ch = getchar();

        putchar(ch);

        return 0;
    }


If user enters:

    A

Output:

    A


This is the foundation of character-by-character processing.


============================================================
21. scanf()
============================================================

scanf() is used for formatted input.

Syntax:

    scanf("format", addresses_of_variables);


Example:

    int age;

    scanf("%d", &age);


Important:

    &age

provides the address of age.


scanf() needs an address so that it can store the input value
in the variable.


============================================================
22. WHY & IS USED WITH scanf()
============================================================

Suppose:

    int age;

Memory conceptually:

    +-------------+
    |      ?      |
    +-------------+
          ^
          |
        age


scanf():

    scanf("%d", &age);


passes the address of age.

scanf() can then store:

    22

inside that memory location.


This is an early and important application of pointers.


============================================================
23. scanf() FOR INTEGER
============================================================

Example:

    int age;

    printf("Enter age: ");
    scanf("%d", &age);

    printf("Age = %d\n", age);


User:

    22


Output:

    Age = 22


============================================================
24. scanf() FOR FLOAT
============================================================

Example:

    float marks;

    scanf("%f", &marks);


Important:

For scanf():

    float  → %f


============================================================
25. scanf() FOR DOUBLE
============================================================

Example:

    double salary;

    scanf("%lf", &salary);


Important:

For scanf():

    float  → %f
    double → %lf


This is one of the most frequently asked C interview
questions.


============================================================
26. scanf() FOR CHARACTER
============================================================

Example:

    char grade;

    scanf(" %c", &grade);


Notice the space before:

    %c


Why?

Because scanf(" %c", ...) tells scanf() to skip leading
whitespace characters before reading the character.


============================================================
27. scanf() FOR STRING
============================================================

Example:

    char name[50];

    scanf("%49s", name);


Notice:

    name

not:

    &name


For an array, the array expression normally converts to a
pointer to its first element.


However, scanf("%s", name) reads only one whitespace-delimited
word.


Input:

    Apoorv Singh


Result:

    name = "Apoorv"


"Singh" remains in the input stream.


============================================================
28. WHY scanf("%s", name) CAN BE DANGEROUS
============================================================

This is dangerous:

    char name[10];

    scanf("%s", name);


If user enters more than 9 characters, scanf() may write beyond
the array bounds.

That can cause:

    - buffer overflow
    - memory corruption
    - security vulnerabilities
    - undefined behavior


Safer:

    scanf("%9s", name);


But for general line input, fgets() is usually preferable.


============================================================
29. fgets()
============================================================

fgets() reads a line/string from a stream.

Syntax:

    fgets(buffer, size, stdin);


Example:

    char name[50];

    fgets(name, sizeof name, stdin);


This is generally safer than an unrestricted %s scanf() for
reading text lines because the buffer size is supplied.


============================================================
30. fgets() EXAMPLE
============================================================

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        char name[50];

        printf("Enter your name: ");

        if (fgets(name, sizeof name, stdin) != NULL)
        {
            printf("Hello, %s", name);
        }

        return 0;
    }


Input:

    Apoorv Singh


Output:

    Hello, Apoorv Singh


Unlike %s, fgets() can read spaces.


============================================================
31. IMPORTANT: fgets() MAY STORE '\n'
============================================================

Suppose user enters:

    Apoorv\n


fgets() may store:

    'A' 'p' 'o' 'o' 'r' 'v' '\n' '\0'


Therefore output formatting can sometimes look unexpected.


============================================================
32. REMOVING NEWLINE FROM fgets()
============================================================

Common approach:

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <string.h>

    char name[50];

    if (fgets(name, sizeof name, stdin) != NULL)
    {
        name[strcspn(name, "\n")] = '\0';
    }


Then:

    "Apoorv\n"

becomes:

    "Apoorv"


This technique uses:

    strcspn()

from:

    <string.h>


Strings will be studied deeply in Chapter 11.


============================================================
33. puts()
============================================================

puts() writes a string followed by a newline.

Example:

    puts("Hello World");


Output:

    Hello World


Equivalent conceptually to:

    printf("%s\n", "Hello World");


But puts() is specifically designed for strings.


============================================================
34. fputs()
============================================================

fputs() writes a string to a specified stream.

Example:

    fputs("Hello", stdout);


Unlike puts(), fputs() does NOT automatically append a newline.


Example:

    fputs("Hello\n", stdout);


