============================================================
36. MULTIPLE INTEGER INPUT
============================================================

Example:

    int a, b;

    printf("Enter two numbers: ");
    scanf("%d %d", &a, &b);

Input:

    10 20


Output:

    a = 10
    b = 20


Whitespace in scanf format generally matches zero or more
whitespace characters in the input.


============================================================
37. INPUT WITH NEWLINE
============================================================

You can enter:

    10 20


or:

    10
    20


For:

    scanf("%d %d", &a, &b);


Both can work because whitespace in the format string allows
whitespace in the input.


============================================================
38. RETURN VALUE OF scanf()
============================================================

Very important.

scanf() returns the number of input items successfully
assigned.

Example:

    int age;

    int result = scanf("%d", &age);


If user enters:

    25

then:

    result == 1


If user enters:

    abc

then:

    result == 0


If input ends before any assignment:

    result == EOF


This is extremely useful for input validation.


============================================================
39. INPUT VALIDATION WITH scanf()
============================================================

Bad:

    scanf("%d", &age);


Better:

    if (scanf("%d", &age) == 1)
    {
        printf("Valid input\n");
    }
    else
    {
        printf("Invalid input\n");
    }


This checks whether an integer was successfully read.


============================================================
40. THE INPUT BUFFER
============================================================

Suppose:

    int age;

    scanf("%d", &age);


User enters:

    25\n


scanf("%d", &age) consumes:

    25


The newline:

    \n

may remain in the input stream.


This becomes important when the next operation reads a
character.


============================================================
41. CLASSIC scanf() + getchar() PROBLEM
============================================================

Example:

    int age;
    char ch;

    scanf("%d", &age);

    ch = getchar();


User enters:

    25\n


scanf("%d", &age) reads:

    25


The newline remains.

Then:

    getchar()

reads:

    '\n'


Therefore ch may contain newline instead of the character
the user intended.


============================================================
42. SOLUTION FOR CHARACTER INPUT
============================================================

Use:

    scanf(" %c", &ch);


The leading space tells scanf() to skip whitespace.


Example:

    int age;
    char grade;

    scanf("%d", &age);
    scanf(" %c", &grade);


Now input such as:

    22
    A


can be handled correctly.


============================================================
43. IMPORTANT: DO NOT USE fflush(stdin)
============================================================

Many beginner tutorials suggest:

    fflush(stdin);


Do NOT use this as a portable way to clear keyboard input.

The behavior of fflush() is defined for output streams; using
fflush(stdin) to discard unread input is not portable C.


Instead:

    - design input carefully
    - validate scanf() results
    - use fgets() for line-oriented input
    - parse the resulting string when necessary


============================================================
44. BETTER INPUT STRATEGY
============================================================

For robust programs, a good general strategy is:

    1. Read a complete line using fgets()
    2. Validate/parse the text
    3. Convert it into the required type


Useful functions include:

    strtol()
    strtoul()
    strtoll()
    strtod()


These are available through:

    <stdlib.h>


This approach gives much better control over malformed input.


============================================================
45. scanf() vs fgets()
============================================================

scanf():

    Good for:
        simple formatted input
        basic exercises
        controlled input

    Problems:
        leftover input
        difficult error recovery
        %s buffer overflow if width omitted
        newline/whitespace surprises


fgets():

    Good for:
        reading complete lines
        user-facing programs
        safer string input
        controlled parsing


Professional programs often prefer:

    fgets()
    +
    strtol()/strtod()/other parsing functions


for robust text input.


============================================================
46. READING AN INTEGER WITH fgets() + strtol()
============================================================

Example:

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <stdlib.h>
    #include <errno.h>
    #include <limits.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        char buffer[100];
        char *end;
        long value;

        printf("Enter an integer: ");

        if (fgets(buffer, sizeof buffer, stdin) == NULL)
            return 1;

        errno = 0;
        value = strtol(buffer, &end, 10);

        if (end == buffer)
        {
            printf("No number was entered.\n");
            return 1;
        }

        if (errno == ERANGE ||
            value < INT_MIN ||
            value > INT_MAX)
        {
            printf("Number is out of int range.\n");
            return 1;
        }

        printf("You entered: %ld\n", value);

        return 0;
    }


This is more advanced and closer to robust production-style
input handling.


============================================================
47. getchar() FOR LINE INPUT
------------------------------------------------------------

You can manually read characters:

    int ch;

    while ((ch = getchar()) != '\n' && ch != EOF)
    {
        ...
    }


This pattern is often used when processing or discarding
characters from an input stream.


============================================================
48. EOF
============================================================

EOF means:

    End Of File


It is a special negative integer value returned by input
functions when the end of an input stream is reached or an
input error occurs, depending on the function/context.


Example:

    int ch;

    while ((ch = getchar()) != EOF)
    {
        putchar(ch);
    }


This program copies standard input to standard output until
EOF is received.


On many terminals:

    Linux/macOS:
        Ctrl+D

    Windows console:
        Ctrl+Z followed by Enter


These are terminal conventions, not C language syntax.


============================================================
49. stderr
============================================================

Errors should often be written to stderr.

Example:

    fprintf(stderr, "Error: Invalid input\n");


Why?

Because stdout and stderr are separate streams and can be
redirected independently.


Example:

    fprintf(stdout, "Normal output\n");
    fprintf(stderr, "Error output\n");


============================================================
50. fprintf()
============================================================

fprintf() is like printf(), but allows you to specify the
output stream.

Syntax:

    fprintf(stream, "format", arguments);


Example:

    fprintf(stdout, "Hello\n");

    fprintf(stderr, "Error occurred\n");


Later, the same function is useful for file I/O.


============================================================
51. fscanf()
============================================================

fscanf() is similar to scanf(), but reads from a specified
stream.

Syntax:

    fscanf(stream, "format", ...);


Example:

    fscanf(stdin, "%d", &age);


Conceptually similar to:

    scanf("%d", &age);


It becomes particularly useful with files.


============================================================
52. fgetc()
============================================================

fgetc() reads one character from a stream.

Example:

    int ch;

    ch = fgetc(stdin);


Similar idea to:

    getchar();


getchar() can be thought of as character input from stdin.


============================================================
53. fputc()
============================================================

fputc() writes one character to a stream.

Example:

    fputc('A', stdout);


Output:

    A


