============================================================
67. BUFFER OVERFLOW
============================================================

Buffer:

    A fixed-size region of memory used to store data.


Example:

    char name[10];


It can hold at most:

    9 normal characters + '\0'

when storing a C string.


If input exceeds the available space and isn't properly
bounded, memory outside the array may be overwritten.


This is a major security issue.


============================================================
68. FORMAT STRING VULNERABILITY
============================================================

Never do:

    printf(user_input);


if user_input is controlled by an untrusted user.

Why?

printf() interprets the string as a format string.

Safer:

    printf("%s", user_input);


This is an important security concept.


============================================================
69. EXAMPLE OF UNSAFE CODE
============================================================

Dangerous:

    char input[100];

    fgets(input, sizeof input, stdin);

    printf(input);


Better:

    printf("%s", input);


The format string should normally be controlled by the
programmer, not arbitrary user input.


============================================================
70. OUTPUT BUFFERING
============================================================

stdout may be buffered.

This means output may not immediately appear on the terminal
depending on buffering mode.

Example:

    printf("Hello");


A newline often helps in terminal interaction:

    printf("Hello\n");


You can explicitly flush stdout:

    fflush(stdout);


This is valid because stdout is an output stream.


============================================================
71. INTERACTIVE PROMPT
============================================================

Example:

    printf("Enter value: ");
    fflush(stdout);


Flushing can ensure the prompt is displayed before a blocking
input operation in environments where stdout is buffered.


============================================================
72. fprintf(stderr, ...)
============================================================

For diagnostics:

    fprintf(stderr, "Error: File not found\n");


This is preferable to mixing errors with normal output when
stream separation matters.


============================================================
73. COMMON scanf() MISTAKE #1
============================================================

Wrong:

    int age;

    scanf("%d", age);


Correct:

    scanf("%d", &age);


Because scanf() needs the address where it should store the
result.


============================================================
74. COMMON scanf() MISTAKE #2
============================================================

Wrong:

    double salary;

    scanf("%f", &salary);


Correct:

    scanf("%lf", &salary);


For scanf:

    float  → %f
    double → %lf


============================================================
75. COMMON scanf() MISTAKE #3
============================================================

Wrong:

    char name[20];

    scanf("%s", &name);


For %s, the expected argument is a pointer to the first
character.

Correct:

    scanf("%19s", name);


Do not confuse:

    name

with:

    &name

They have different pointer types.


============================================================
76. COMMON scanf() MISTAKE #4
============================================================

Wrong:

    char ch;

    scanf("%c", &ch);

after previous numeric input, when the intention is to read
the next non-whitespace character.

Better:

    scanf(" %c", &ch);


============================================================
77. COMMON scanf() MISTAKE #5
============================================================

Dangerous:

    char name[10];

    scanf("%s", name);


Safer:

    scanf("%9s", name);


Even better for line input:

    fgets(name, sizeof name, stdin);


============================================================
78. COMMON scanf() MISTAKE #6
============================================================

Wrong assumption:

    scanf() always successfully reads the requested value.


Not true.

Always consider:

    scanf(...) == expected_number_of_assignments


Example:

    if (scanf("%d", &x) != 1)
    {
        printf("Invalid integer\n");
    }


============================================================
79. printf() vs puts()
============================================================

printf():

    formatted output

Example:

    printf("Age = %d\n", age);


puts():

    simple string output + newline

Example:

    puts("Hello");


Use printf() when formatting is required.


============================================================
80. getchar() vs scanf("%c")
============================================================

Both can read characters, but they behave differently in
important ways because scanf() processes input according to
its format string.

getchar():

    reads one character from stdin.


scanf("%c", &ch):

    also reads one character, including whitespace.


scanf(" %c", &ch):

    skips leading whitespace and then reads a character.


============================================================
81. scanf() vs getchar()
============================================================

scanf():

    formatted conversion


getchar():

    one character


Example:

    int ch = getchar();


This may read:

    'A'

or:

    '\n'

or:

    EOF


============================================================
82. fgets() vs gets()
============================================================

gets() was historically available but is unsafe because it
cannot know the size of the destination buffer.

It was removed from the C standard in C11.

Never use:

    gets()


Use:

    fgets()


instead.


============================================================
83. ROBUST LINE INPUT PATTERN
============================================================

    char buffer[100];

    if (fgets(buffer, sizeof buffer, stdin) == NULL)
    {
        fprintf(stderr, "Input error or EOF\n");
        return 1;
    }


Then process the contents.


This is a strong pattern to remember for job interviews.


============================================================
84. COMPLETE INPUT VALIDATION EXAMPLE
============================================================

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        int age;

        printf("Enter your age: ");

        if (scanf("%d", &age) != 1)
        {
            fprintf(stderr, "Invalid integer input.\n");
            return 1;
        }

        if (age < 0 || age > 150)
        {
            fprintf(stderr, "Age out of valid range.\n");
            return 1;
        }

        printf("Valid age: %d\n", age);

        return 0;
    }


