============================================================
87. PROFESSIONAL INPUT PRINCIPLE
============================================================

Never trust external input.

External input includes:

    user input
    file input
    network input
    command-line arguments
    environment variables
    API data


Always consider:

    - Is it syntactically valid?
    - Is it within range?
    - Is it too long?
    - Is it malicious?
    - Is conversion successful?


This principle becomes extremely important in
software development and security.


============================================================
88. IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE:
    FORMAT STRING vs ARGUMENT
============================================================

Example:

    printf("Age = %d", age);


Format string:

    "Age = %d"


Argument:

    age


The format specifier tells printf() how to interpret the
corresponding argument.


============================================================
89. FORMAT STRING AND TYPE SAFETY
============================================================

C's printf()/scanf() interfaces are variadic functions.

The compiler cannot always fully check the relationship
between format strings and arguments.

Modern compilers can detect many mistakes when format strings
are literal and warnings are enabled.

Compile with warnings:

    gcc -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic program.c -o program


Warnings are extremely important in professional C.


============================================================
90. COMPILER WARNING EXAMPLE
============================================================

Potential mistake:

    int x = 10;

    printf("%f\n", x);


The format does not match the argument type expected by
printf().

Compile with warnings:

    gcc -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic program.c -o program


Compiler diagnostics can help identify the problem.


============================================================
91. COMMAND-LINE INPUT PREVIEW
============================================================

C programs can receive arguments from the command line.

Example:

    int main(int argc, char *argv[])
    {
        ...
    }


Command:

    ./program Apoorv


Then:

    argc
    argv


contain command-line information.

This will be studied in:

    Chapter 19 — Command-Line Arguments


============================================================
92. I/O ERROR HANDLING
============================================================

Input/output operations can fail.

Examples:

    EOF
    invalid input
    stream error
    interrupted operation
    file error


Many I/O functions provide return values that should be
checked.


Examples:

    scanf()
    fgets()
    getchar()
    printf()
    fprintf()


Do not ignore return values when correctness matters.


============================================================
93. printf() ERROR CHECKING
============================================================

printf() returns a negative value if an output error occurs.

Example:

    if (printf("Hello\n") < 0)
    {
        fprintf(stderr, "Output error\n");
    }


In simple programs this is often omitted, but robust software
should consider I/O errors where appropriate.


============================================================
94. BUFFERED vs UNBUFFERED CONCEPT
============================================================

Streams can be buffered.

Common buffering modes include:

    fully buffered
    line buffered
    unbuffered


The exact behavior depends on the stream and environment.

Understanding buffering becomes important for:

    terminal applications
    logging
    files
    pipes
    networking


============================================================
95. JOB-READY I/O RULES
============================================================

Rule 1:

    Always match format specifier with the type.


Rule 2:

    Use & for scalar variables with scanf().


Rule 3:

    Do not use & before an array name for %s.


Rule 4:

    Prefer fgets() for line-oriented string input.


Rule 5:

    Never use gets().


Rule 6:

    Do not use fflush(stdin) as a portable input-clearing
    technique.


Rule 7:

    Check scanf() return value.


Rule 8:

    Validate ranges.


Rule 9:

    Never trust user input.


Rule 10:

    Compile with warnings.


